_irNotes: {
	"decision-01": "01 — everything is tab, and the grader is the reason. No Postgres, no Electric, no conduit, no bloblang, no migration, no policy, no publication, no pipeline. The reason is not the size: for an app whose oracle IS arithmetic, putting the arithmetic in a seat no fixture harness reaches destroys the grader before it is written. A reduce at the mutation seat is the one seat an off-disk mount executes. Nothing is at device either — a device collection persists across program versions and nothing backfills a new column, and this schema is certain to move"
	"decision-02": "02 — the seat is pinned on a row declared last. A data-on-mutation seat wakes when its OWN region's read changes and at no other time. The seed is written collection by collection when the tab store is first opened, so a seat pinned on Token would fold over a world with no Reader rows in it and produce nothing, with nothing red. Fold is declared last and carries one row, so the seat's wake is the last thing the seeding does"
	"decision-03": "03 — the arithmetic is written twice and the two files share nothing. shell/handlers/audit.js and tests/ground-truth.ts both implement sRGB-to-linear, the Machado matrix multiply, OKLab and the Euclidean distance, from the published model rather than from each other. A shared helper would turn the agreement into a tautology, and the agreement is the grade. The same reasoning splits fixtures/generate.ts from the oracle's parser: the fixture check compares two parses of the same bytes"
	"decision-04": "04 — the separation is not finished until a browser has painted it. The five checks that run at test mount into linkedom and compute no style, so none of them can tell a painted specimen from an empty box. paint reads getComputedStyle off the specimen elements over the running cluster and recomputes the ΔE from what was resolved. The dark half is reached the way the platform resolves appearance — through the context's colour scheme — never by toggling a class"
	"decision-05": "05 — the matrix is nine integer columns at ten-thousandths. A reduce has no matrix library, so the model has to arrive as data. Integers rather than floats because the two routes are held to agree EXACTLY at hundredths, and a float column would put a rounding argument between them. It also puts the model on the specification screen, where a reviewer can check that the app simulates what it says it simulates"
	"decision-06": "06 — a pair whose two members never differ is shown and not graded. Three of the twenty-two pairs the segment rule admits resolve to the same bytes in all fourteen themes: the diff-blob inks. The ground carries that opposition and the ink does not. Grading them would report a zero separation as a failure in every theme and say nothing about any of them, and dropping them silently would hide a fact about the system. They are rows with their reason on them"
	"decision-07": "07 — the simulated colours are columns on the reading, not a second set of rows. The reader control has to repaint the SAME elements — a second set of pre-simulated rows swapped in would make the simulation the fixture's and not the app's, and paint would be grading a transcription. So each reading carries the two colours as that reader sees them, computed by the app's own arithmetic, and the picker moves which reading is painted"
	"decision-08": "08 — the claim and the reading are different columns, and the probe is a third. says is the vendor's own sentence, quoted out of the vendored guide by a substring match that raises if the guide does not carry it. reads is what this app computes. They sit in adjacent cells and are never merged, so rewording the app's reading to match the specification would be a diff on a column that is supposed to be a quotation. probe is empty exactly where the claim is the vendor's, and carries the unification for every language finding — which is the difference between this and a hand-written table of things a vocabulary cannot say"
	"decision-09": "09 — the chart palette is counted and declined, with the number as the reason. Primer's seventeen categorical chart colours are constant within each ground across all fourteen themes, and their worst all-pairs separation is under a third of a unit for every reader. So the reader axis never reaches them, and grading a seventeen-slot categorical set would be vacuous in the other direction — the same failure in all fourteen themes. The app records the number rather than grading it"
	"decision-10": "10 — no verdict is carried by colour, and that is a consequence rather than a taste. The app's own audit found no theme in its subject that survives all three readers. A tool that encoded its answer in a hue would therefore be unreadable to one of the three readers it serves, and its own subject is the proof. Every verdict on every screen is a word, and paint asserts that no two verdict elements differ only in computed colour"
}

